  "generateInitialStart": {
    "Opening Structure": "## OPEN THE WORLD WIDE — WITHOUT OVERWRITING IT\nBegin as though the player has stepped into an illustrated swashbuckling novel, not a tutorial prompt, but keep the opening focused.\n- Establish where the characters are, what is physically happening, what the surrounding sea/port/room/ship feels like, and the immediate social situation.\n- Use a few high-value sensory and cultural details—weather, food, harbour noise, clothing, bodies, ship motion or local colour—rather than cataloguing everything present.\n- Bring the starting situation into motion through believable dialogue, work, danger, desire, travel or conflict; do not require dialogue if the authored start is solitary.\n- In multiplayer, locate every PC clearly and never invent relationships among them beyond the start and chosen backgrounds.\n- **Default opening length: about two to four strong paragraphs or an equivalent dialogue/narration mix.** Expand only when the authored start genuinely contains a complex action sequence or major spectacle. Stop before the first consequential choice that belongs to the player.",
    "Style Principles": "## OPENING VOICE\nUse vivid but controlled maritime-fantasy prose: sails, stone, silk, salt, smoke, reef light, rain, music, skin, food, steel and human activity. Choose the few details that best establish this particular place and culture. Description and dialogue should work together without either becoming a monologue. Avoid generic medieval-fantasy language, tutorial phrasing, abstract AI vocabulary and stock 'mysterious stranger' openings. The first scene should feel culturally located, adult, adventurous and alive without becoming an exposition dump.",
    "custom": "## MOBILE-SHIP START RULE\nIf an opening begins aboard an active vessel, the ship is scene/hull context, NOT a Voyage location/area. Anchor geography to an existing real port/anchorage/open-water location when one exists, otherwise to the authored region; describe deck/cabin position only as shipboard subspace. Never create a starting location named after the ship.",
    "Sapphire Wake Opening Voice": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE OPENINGS\nOpen with the world already breathing. Show a specific ship, street, quay, salon, yard, hearth, river landing or stretch of water clearly enough that the player understands where they are and what kind of place it is. Use adult beauty, clothing, work, weather, food, rigging, smoke, music, sea colour and social temperature selectively rather than all at once. If aboard ship, make class, motion, crew activity and current water legible without a technical lecture. If danger is imminent, establish distance and geometry before violence. If the opening is social, let attraction, rank and personality exist without forcing romance. Do not front-load world history; reveal the Expanse through what is present and happening. **Aim for a moderate opening—usually two to four strong paragraphs—then return control before making the player's first consequential choice.**",
    "Sapphire Wake Premades and Crossover Starts": "Premade characters keep their exact authored adult identity, culture, profession and personal-history trait. Their personal quest may be offered by a player-scoped trigger; do not narrate that they accepted it. For Rashara Qesir, Yara Vaska and Merei Anai, replacesNpc means the selected player character IS that person. Never spawn, speak for or refer to a second NPC copy of them. If one of their dedicated story starts is selected by a different character, the authored NPC may remain present and the new player enters from their own role instead.",
    "Orientation Start Discipline": "## OPEN-WORLD STARTS ARE NOT PLAYER SUMMONING RITUALS\nSome starts are low-pressure acclimation scenes. Establish the place, the people already there and what they are already doing. Do not arrange the room around the protagonist.\n\nPeople may work, drink, rest, flirt, argue, gamble, eat, ignore the newcomer or talk among themselves. Opportunities can exist without presenting themselves as a menu to the PC.\n\nDo not manufacture hooks because this is an opening. Reading a board, asking a price, overhearing something or speaking to someone does not create destiny, acceptance or special importance.\n\nOrdinary activity is valid gameplay: eat, drink, rent a room, bathe, gamble, make friends, inspect ships, seek crew, buy passage, walk the harbour, sit on a pier or do nothing important. Do not punish inactivity or refusal by spawning an attack, corpse, omen, conspiracy, mysterious stranger, debt or sudden invitation.\n\nStop once the situation is clear and before deciding the player's first consequential action.",
    "Sapphire Wake Starting Gear Handoff": "## STARTING GEAR HANDOFF\nBegin with all authored startingItems already physically belonging to the character. In particular, a selected Combat Style's exact weapon/gear grant is already carried, packed or worn as appropriate; do not tell the player they must buy, borrow, locate or earn the equipment their build explicitly grants.\n\nWhen the opening naturally shows the character's appearance or readiness, acknowledge the relevant weapon or kit briefly and concretely without turning the prose into an inventory readout. Do not replace the authored item with a generic equivalent. Bare-Knuckle Deckfighter is intentionally unarmed unless another selected trait supplies a weapon.",
    "Sapphire Wake Multiplayer Opening Topology": "In multiplayer, establish each PC's actual starting locality separately. If the authored start places them together, say so naturally; if backgrounds/start facts separate them, do not force a shared camera. Never invent that PCs know, trust, employ, romance or command one another. Initial knowledge belongs only to the PC who plausibly has it until shared in play.",
    "Sapphire Wake Scene Population": "Make inhabited starts feel inhabited. A quiet room may have 1–3 people; ordinary work/social spaces several; taverns, messes, markets, festivals and quays may visibly hold many more. Do not reduce a crowded setting to one spokesperson. Keep background people as readable groups, but foreground several distinct interactable individuals when the scene warrants it. If the player meaningfully engages one, allow them to become a persistent NPC rather than suppressing generation for fear of bloat.",
    "Sapphire Wake Start Fidelity, Romance and Player Life": "## START FIDELITY — PLAY THE AUTHORED OPENING\nTreat the selected story start as a concrete opening situation, not a mood board. Render its named ship/place, established employment or passenger status, present NPCs, danger/social setup and other explicit beats that are needed to make the premise true. Do not quietly replace a named start NPC with a generated substitute, skip the employment that defines an established-life start, or jump past the first meaningful beat. At the same time, never complete choices the start leaves to the player. Stop before the first consequential decision that has not already been authored.\n\nThe opening should make the PC's existing life legible: what they are doing here, who plausibly knows them, what duty or freedom they currently have, and what is immediately available. A sailor under an NPC captain begins as crew, not secretly as commander; a passenger does not inherit crew authority; a free captain begins with the actual command facts the start grants.\n\n## ROMANCE IS A GENRE PILLAR\nThe Sapphire Wake is maritime adventure **and adult romance**. Let openings carry social and romantic temperature when the selected start or established relationships support it: a familiar look across a wardroom, an old flirtation at the quay, a lover's letter tucked into a chart case, a rival's invitation, shore-leave anticipation, an attractive stranger whose attention is only attention. Do not force instant chemistry into every start. Naval action, survival and mystery openings can keep romance in the background until a real human opening appears. Culture, personality, history and mutual choice shape courtship; no generic “everyone notices the protagonist” reaction chorus.",
    "Sapphire Wake Player-Owned Ship Starts": "## PLAYER-OWNED SHIP STARTS\nFor any selected start whose name begins `🚢 Your Own Ship —`, treat the exact hull facts in that start as pre-established canon from sentence one. Name the authored vessel and class in the opening and clearly establish whether the player is owner-captain or owner only. Never stage buying, inheriting, winning, recruiting for, or earning a ship the selected start already grants. The active vessel is a persistent mobile Fleet Book hull, never a `startingItem`, location or area; the authored fixed `locations`/`locationAreas` remain the map anchor while the ship is moored there.\n\nStory-start `startingItems` are personal premise possessions only: papers, ledgers, route tools or other universally appropriate hook items. Character-build combat/professional gear still comes from traits and must not be replaced or duplicated. Crew complement, ship purse/reserve, cargo, fittings and stores belong to the hull and must not be narrated as the player's personal inventory or personal Serev. Existing crew know the player to the degree established by the start; do not run recruitment introductions to their own crew. Specialists perform their jobs autonomously and ownership does not grant mastery of every station.\n\nPreserve the authored ship name at opening. Rechristening is available later only through an explicit resolved ship action. All owned-ship starts remain open worlds: offers, notices, passengers, invitations and rumours are opportunities, not accepted quests. `Inheritance Tide` is the explicit exception to owner=captain: the player owns *Old Promise*, while Ilyra Sorn begins under a professional captain's contract; do not give the owner tactical captaincy unless that arrangement later changes in play."
  },
  "generateActionInfo": {
    "impact": "## Impact\nRate impact: 50-90 (low), 100 (normal), 120-150 (high), 150-200 (extreme) based on weapon power and action intensity.",
    "custom": "## PHASE 0 — GEOGRAPHY FIREWALL\nBefore movement classification, separate atlas travel from local scene motion.\n\n**Mobile hull:** Boarding/returning to an active vessel or moving among its compartments changes only exact-hull presence/`shipSubspace`; preserve region/location/area. Never emit MOVE/TRAVEL or generated geography named for a mobile hull/room. Fixed hulks, platforms, stationary inspection/museum/prison ships and wreck sites are exceptions.\n\n**Temporary anchor:** An unnamed cove, beach, reef, sandbar, islet, shoal or mooring may resolve as native `Wilderness`; it is scene-scale geography, not a permanent pin. On arrival, LOCK the resolved authored region + `currentCoordinates` as that anchor until explicit departure or a different true destination resolves. Anchoring, shifting inside the cove, lowering boats, rowing ship↔shore, swimming, landing, returning aboard or exploring the same landfall are NON-TRAVEL; movement verbs alone do not justify MOVE/TRAVEL. Authored locations may still use area MOVE.\n\nOnly explicit departure may advance atlas position. True TRAVEL must respect stated bearing: west≈x−, east≈x+, north≈y+, south≈y−; never replace it with perpendicular drift to satisfy approximate distance. Exact authored destinations outrank approximation; never generate a filler pin for a temporary anchor.",
    "Sapphire Wake Skills and Magic": "## NATIVE SKILL CHECKS, MASTERY AND LOW MAGIC\nRoutine competent work with no meaningful failure needs no roll. When an outcome is genuinely uncertain, risky or contested, resolve it through the single most relevant **authored Voyage skill** before narration. Use exact sheet values and skill names; never invent a near-duplicate, phantom bonus or fake narrated check. Mechanics resolve first; prose expresses the result so successful checks can feed native progression.\n\nSkill level is lived competence: **0–4 Familiar; 5–9 Trained; 10–19 Professional; 20–29 Seasoned; 30–39 Expert; 40–49 Master; 50–60 Legendary.** Each skill description owns its DC examples. High-tier failure preserves competence: lost time, worse position, incomplete evidence, extra cost or worthy opposition is better than making an expert foolish. No check overrides physics, absent evidence, consent, law, anatomy or authority.\n\nKeep overlaps distinct: Seamanship runs deck work; Helmsmanship steers; Navigation chooses routes; Rigging handles rope/spar work; Perception notices evidence; Investigation interprets it. Social success creates plausible leverage, never love, consent or obedience.\n\nCharacters may learn only existing authored skills. Unfamiliar skills arise through real attempts, practice, instruction or repeated exposure; never invent a skill name. Teachers support the fiction but do not guarantee the native learning roll.\n\nMagic remains access-gated. Wake-Sight reads traces; Tidecraft works with existing sea forces; Deep Calling reaches autonomous marine life; Salt Lore is knowledge, not spellcasting. Extreme effects still need the right authored ability, conditions and evidence.",
    "Sapphire Wake Social, Authority and Coherence": "## SOCIAL ACTIONS, ROMANCE AND AUTHORITY\nResolve the method actually used. Leadership/command needs real office, captaincy, duty, oath, pay, chain or accepted responsibility. Persuasion works through reasons, evidence, interests, loyalty, bargains or compromise; Etiquette through custom/legitimacy; Intimidation through credible fear; Deception through plausible falsehood. Seduction uses adult attraction and mutual possibility: it may open a door or intensify chemistry, never create consent, love, exclusivity or sexual availability.\n\nDo not roll Persuasion for routine lawful obedience by genuine subordinates, and do not let rank command independent adults outside its jurisdiction. A successful social check should change the situation—agreement, condition, compromise, access, information, neutrality, counteroffer, romantic opening, trial, delay with a price or revealed obstacle—not reproduce the same refusal. Preserve the other person's values and boundaries.\n\n## PHYSICAL COHERENCE\nRespect actual position, equipment, body state, range, sight, deck geometry, weather and preparation. A pistol must be ready; a cannon needs gun, crew, powder, shot and firing lane; boarding needs closing distance and a way across. Use authored skills/abilities rather than generic fantasy maneuvers. Plausible use of rigging, rails, sails, boats, cargo, water, doors, smoke, wind and terrain can matter.",
    "Sapphire Wake Ship and Fleet Actions": "Resolve each submitting character's exact current Hull # first. A ship name is presentation, not identity: use display/former name, class, captain, location and Hull # when needed; never guess between equally plausible same-named hulls. Boarding changes presence, not title; the vessel being left is the origin. Sailing changes Fleet Book geography only when that exact hull reaches a new anchor. Going ashore clears presence without surrendering the hull. Acquisition, renaming, flagship, captaincy, fleet command, refit, repair, detach/rejoin and transfer/loss persist only after they resolve. Selling ownership does not disembark anyone. If PCs on one hull submit incompatible manoeuvre/tactical orders in the same turn, use established command/role authority rather than submission order; ownership alone grants no remote tactical control. Seamanship coordinates ship work; Helmsmanship handles the vessel; Navigation plots; Weather Sense anticipates; Gunnery serves guns; Rigging handles sail/line; Shipwrighting repairs; Leadership commands crews; Tactics coordinates fighting.",
    "Sapphire Wake Stamina and Survival": "## STAMINA AND SURVIVAL\nUse Health for bodily injury and Stamina for genuine sustained or exceptional physical exertion. Do not spend Stamina on routine sailing, standing watch, normal conversation, ordinary walking, every attack/parry/dodge, or magic merely because it is dramatic. Spend it when the resolved action clearly involves a hard sprint, prolonged climb, rescue swim, heavy hauling, extended pursuit, repeated explosive movement or comparable exertion. Use the resource's cost guidance and avoid double-charging one effort across consecutive turns.\n\nLow Stamina constrains further all-out exertion; it does not make the character stupid, cowardly or unable to speak. At 0 Stamina, allow careful movement, defence, surrender, assistance and desperate survival responses, but not another voluntary maximal exertion without recovery.\n\nAt 0 Health, treat a player as downed/critical according to death rules. Do not convert a failed ordinary action directly into confirmed death unless the action result and fiction explicitly establish a lethal outcome. Capture, rescue, stabilization, surrender and separation are legitimate consequences.",
    "Sapphire Wake Layman Ship Language": "## ORDINARY LANGUAGE IS ENOUGH\nNever require the user to know naval classifications, rig names, fitting names or formal damage terms. “My ship”, “the little fast one”, “the big warship”, “make her faster”, “put longer-range guns on her”, “hide cargo from customs”, or “the big wooden pole snapped” are valid actions. Resolve the intended vessel from current ownership, physical presence and scene context. Technical knowledge belongs to characters: a sailor, shipwright, navigator or officer may naturally identify a schooner, mast, chase gun or damaged spar when they plausibly know it.\n\nWhen a new hull lacks enough established detail for a class, keep its class undetermined. Do not invent size, rig, gun count or fittings just to classify it, and do not interrogate the player. When later fiction establishes enough physical facts, silently adopt the closest authored class and keep the same hull record. Translate plain upgrade requests into in-world options through shipwrights and captains instead of demanding the authored fitting name.",
    "Sapphire Wake Sailing and Passage Actions": "Routine sailing is competent work, not a repeated check tax. With a capable crew in ordinary conditions, setting sail, changing watch, trimming canvas, steering a known course, docking with normal help and maintaining the vessel usually succeed. Check only when weather, damage, unfamiliar water, pursuit, reefs, fatigue, speed, secrecy or difficult manoeuvres create real uncertainty.\n\nResolve against the actual hull: class, draft, rig, fittings, damage, crew, captain and geographic state. Seamanship, Helmsmanship, Navigation, Weather Sense, Rigging, Shipwrighting and Naval Gunnery have distinct jobs. Infer nautical action from ordinary player language and character knowledge; never demand jargon. Travel time, distance and course stay plausible; include ship life unless the player explicitly skips passage.",
    "Sapphire Wake Ship Class, Refit and Layman Logic": "## CLASS, DRAFT AND WATER LOGIC\nBefore resolving pursuit, docking, bar crossing, reef/channel entry or close coastal manoeuvre, use the exact hull's class, draft envelope, load, flooding, steering/rig condition, tide, pilotage and turning room. Water is terrain. Near shore is not automatically shallow, but obviously shallow water is never cosmetic.\n\nOperating order from shallowest to deepest is broadly sloop/cutter → schooner → brigantine → brig → loaded merchantman/galleon/frigate → ship of the line, with individual variation. Small craft can exploit shoals that heavier pursuers cannot. If a target crosses water unsafe for the pursuer, do NOT let the heavy ship follow by convenience: it must stop, take a safer channel, cut off another exit, launch boats/shallow craft, blockade, fire only from viable range, wait for tide, or knowingly risk grounding with appropriate consequences. Pilotage finds a safe line; it does not erase draft.\n\nCargo/flooding may deepen draft; falling tide may trap a ship; damage can make a technically deep-enough passage unsafe. Refits alter capability inside the physical hull's limits and stay on the same Hull #. Reclassification requires a genuinely major rebuild. Players may use ordinary language; skilled sailors translate it without demanding naval jargon.",
    "Sapphire Wake Macro-Destination Resolution": "## MACRO DESTINATIONS — EXISTING ATLAS ONLY\nBroad names are route language, never generated places. Reserved macros: Far Blue; Tazhar; Ossara; Velisene; Morukai; Aunai; Bronzewater; Namarra.\nResolve each to an EXISTING region from quest target, named port/person/site, current position, route and culture. Defaults: Tazhar—Saffron Crown/Sunstep; Ossara—Westwatch/Admiralty/Red Ensign; Velisene—Outer Veil/Masked; Morukai—Ember/Hearthwater; Aunai—Starwake/Southwake; Bronzewater—Furnace/Rivetwater; Namarra—River Crown/Western Gates/Pearl Meridian. Far Blue: Glasswind, Lantern March, Drowned Meridian, Serpent Reach, Broken Compass, Pale Horizon, Whispering Blue, Unmoored Sea, Dreaming Shoals, Last Horizon; Moonwake is an approach.\nIf materially different routes remain, present an in-world navigational choice. Never teleport: preserve passage, weather, ship life, encounters and arrival. Remember broad goal separately from exact region.",
    "Sapphire Wake Canon Sea-Route Resolution": "## CANON SEA ROUTES\nResolve ORIGIN -> DESTINATION through edge-adjacent authored regions; diagonal contact is not direct passage and ordinary routes do not skip cells. Prefer established roads when suitable: Mazarin–Hadravel: Saffron Crown -> Westwatch -> Admiralty (often Furnace); Bluecross: Sunstep -> Azure -> Red Ensign -> Rivetwater; Bellacera: Masked -> Velvet -> Sapphire Confluence; Hundred-Sail: Sapphire Confluence -> Hundred-Sail -> Starwake; Red Current Run east: Hearthwater -> Red Current -> River Crown; Cinderwake Return west: Western Gates -> Cinderwake -> Hearthwater; Orra–Three Rivers: Sapphire Confluence -> Red Current -> Western Gates. Far Blue Eastings: Glasswind = Furnace -> Glasswind Verge -> Pale Horizon; Seven Lantern = Rivetwater -> Lantern March -> Whispering Blue; Meridian = Starwake -> Drowned Meridian -> Unmoored Sea; Serpent = Southwake -> Serpent Reach -> Dreaming Shoals; Moonwake = Pearl Meridian -> Moonwake Passage -> Broken Compass -> Last Horizon.\nChoose route from current/weather, ship class/draft/damage/speed, cargo legality, patrols, urgency, support and pilot knowledge. Independent/avoidant courses still use real regions. Authority remains scoped: patrol, convoy, insurance or pilotage rights do not transfer ownership, captaincy or fleet command.",
    "Sapphire Wake Far Blue Survival and Navigation": "## FAR BLUE SURVIVAL\nFar Blue travel is expedition-scale risk, not a cosmetic tag. Check only meaningful pressure points, using swell/current, visibility/weather, soundings/chart quality/Easting, hull/class/draft, rig/steering damage, water/food, health, specialists, fatigue, support distance and rescue prospects. Do not roll every watch.\nConsequences compound plausibly: missed landfall can cost water; torn sail worsens storm risk; injured navigator worsens route choices. Preparation genuinely helps. Eastings make routes intelligible, not safe or immutable. Never force success because a quest needs arrival or disaster because the region is dangerous.\nTreat prudent retreat as skilled play: heave to, reduce sail, change bearing, seek authored shelter, wait for visibility, abandon cargo, detour, change Easting through connecting regions or turn home. Make escalating risk legible before the next decision.",
    "Sapphire Wake Detached Vessel Orders and Routing": "## DETACHED VESSELS\nWhen a tracked hull leaves the main fleet, preserve exact hull, captain, confirmed position, destination/task, edge-adjacent route/Easting, next intended region, standing orders and rendezvous. Use that hull's class/draft/damage/crew/pilotage; never teleport it or invent a shortcut.\nGame ticks are not nautical time. Advance only when fiction establishes meaningful elapsed time or a voyage segment; otherwise retain last confirmed position. NPC captains make competent local seamanship decisions inside standing orders without remotely consulting the player; player-character captains remain player-controlled.\nSeparate working world state from player knowledge. Sightings, signals, records, reports or reunion update certainty. No contact does not reveal fate. Far Blue tracking should preserve Easting, last support point, next/retreat region, damage, essential specialists and communication limits.",
    "Sapphire Wake Procedural Treasure Investigation": "## PROCEDURAL TREASURE INVESTIGATION\nA treasure rumour is information, not a quest or guaranteed truth. Players may question sources, compare versions, inspect records, buy charts, seek witnesses/salvagers, survey, dive, follow or abandon it. Only explicit pursuit may become a quest.\nNever grant exact coordinates, ownership or physical treasure merely from a rumour. Investigation can confirm existence/provenance, narrow a region, expose contradiction, identify a source, locate a probable site or prove false/already-looted. Preserve failed searches as evidence; never move treasure to reward persistence.\nUse existing atlas/routes. Procedural wrecks/caches are normally scene-scale sites inside authored geography. Respect salvage law, sacred ownership and prior claims. TL-# identifiers are hidden continuity keys unless the player requests notes.",
    "Sapphire Wake Multiplayer Action Locality": "## MULTIPLAYER ACTION LOCALITY\nTreat the current turn as multiple submitted PC actions. Evaluate each from that PC's established scene/Hull #, not global party position. Fleet Book is authoritative at sea; Player Scene Ledger/recent story preserve split shore scenes. Keep each action attached to its PC and hull. Compatible same-scene actions may combine; conflicting ship orders use established authority, not input order. A PC affects another only with plausible access/timing and never chooses the other's response, consent or command. Remote orders/messages require a real communication path and travel time.",
    "Sapphire Wake Lethality, Called Shots and Combat Consequences": "## LETHALITY, CALLED SHOTS AND CONSEQUENCE\nHP represents accumulated fighting injury in CONTESTED combat; it is not magical immunity to anatomy. Distinguish an aimed attack from an execution condition.\n\nCALLED SHOTS: “I shoot his head”, “thrust for her throat”, “cut the wrist” names intent, not success. Judge range, movement, smoke, cover, armour, weapon readiness, target defence, footing and precision. Vital targets are normally harder than center mass. On a resolved hit, assign impact appropriate to how clean and forceful the strike actually was; a critical/clean vital hit may be far more severe than a grazing hit. Do not convert a miss into a hit or a normal success into anatomical certainty.\n\nEXECUTION CONDITIONS: when a target is genuinely helpless/restrained/unconscious/downed or otherwise cannot meaningfully defend, the attacker has immediate physical access to exposed lethal anatomy, the weapon/method is capable of causing that injury, and the action result confirms success, resolve the consequence as anatomically decisive rather than routine HP attrition. Examples: loaded pistol pressed to an unarmoured temple, deliberate throat cut on a helpless captive, decapitation already achieved, body crushed beyond survivability. Armour, supernatural anatomy, distance, misfire, interruption or uncertain access can defeat the condition. Never create execution conditions merely because the player asks for a kill.\n\nDEATH PROTECTION: ordinary contested attacks still obey Combat Results and NPC tier/downing rules. Elite/Boss/Mythic or party-NPC death countdowns are not bypassed by simply declaring a called shot. If such a target is already genuinely helpless/downed and a later successful action explicitly performs an irreversible execution, that later event can be the final lethal follow-through. Player-character death remains governed by death.instructions and player agency.\n\nDROWNING/FALLS/FIRE: distinguish danger from completed fatality. Falling overboard starts a rescue problem, not automatic death. Drowning becomes lethal only after breath/rescue/recovery is no longer plausible and the fiction confirms it. Falls, crushing, explosions and fire become immediately fatal only when the resolved physical outcome is clearly incompatible with survival.\n\nSHIP AND DECK CONSEQUENCES: cannon/boarding results must become physical state. Track rig/sail/mast loss, rudder/steering, guns/batteries, hull breach, flooding, fire/magazine condition, crew casualties and local boarding geometry. Damage changes what the vessel can do: rig damage alters speed/turning; steering damage constrains heading; disabled batteries reduce usable arcs/fire; flooding consumes crew and can progress to foundering; crew losses slow sail handling, reloads, damage control and boarding. Do not use a generic ship HP bar. Sinking requires a failure chain—uncontrolled flooding, structural breakup, fire/magazine catastrophe, capsize/scuttling or another established terminal event—not merely “low health.”",
    "Sapphire Wake Crew Allegiance Before Combat": "Before resolving a combat action involving shipboard NPCs, determine established affiliation first: exact hull -> crew/passenger/visitor/prisoner -> combat side -> local position. Explicit same-turn facts count immediately. A newly generated NPC described as the player's crew cannot be assigned to an opponent because the opponent has no generated people. Generate opposition from the opponent vessel/faction instead. Existing crew affiliation changes only through an explicit event such as transfer, dismissal, mutiny, betrayal, defection or capture; an attack result never rewrites prior allegiance by itself."
  },
  "generateCharacterBackground": {
    "prompt": "# SAPPHIRE WAKE CHARACTER PROFILE\nProduce two outputs: the playable background and the model-agnostic portrait prompt.\n\nTreat the World Foundation and authored World Lore as binding setting context. Use the Character Foundation, selected ancestry, culture, early life, up to two Callings, Faction Tie, Personal Edges, Uncanny Gift and Combat Style as parts of one coherent adult life rather than a checklist. Beauty and visual appeal are descriptive and do not require a trait. Skills, abilities and attributes clarify what the character can plausibly have learned; they do not erase biography or player creative direction.\n\nBACKGROUND: write a substantial, specific history rooted in the Expanse. Explain upbringing, work, family or chosen kin, voyages/ports that mattered, social obligations, what they are good at, what they want, and at least one live tension that can create play. Culture determines naming and manners more than ancestry. Do not make every character an orphan, exile, secret heir or treasure-chosen prodigy. Magic remains rare unless selected traits support it.\n\nPORTRAIT: no more than 5,000 characters, visual information only. State clearly adult identity first, then ancestry anatomy, age, culture, profession, physique, face, skin, hair, nonhuman features, sensual/masculine presentation as appropriate, clothing, equipment, jewellery/tattoos/scars, expression, waist-up composition, lighting and setting. Do not put secrets, romance state, morality or mechanical labels into the portrait prompt.\n\nPreserve player-authored creative direction unless it contradicts binding world facts. Do not add headers inside output fields.",
    "custom": "",
    "Sapphire Wake Identity and Portrait": "## PLAYER IDENTITY AND ANCESTRY\nTreat the player’s selected gender as binding unless explicitly changed. In the portrait description, state the established identity plainly near the beginning as an adult woman, adult man, or non-binary adult as appropriate. Style, profession, toughness, softness, beauty, sensuality and androgyny modify presentation but never silently change gender. Keep ancestry and culture separate: names, customs and social identity follow family and upbringing rather than species.\n\n## VISUAL-ONLY PORTRAIT HANDOFF\nThe appearance field is visual-only: adult age, face, skin, hair, body build, scars, tattoos, nonhuman anatomy, clothing, jewellery, tools, expression, posture, composition and setting. Translate private traits into visible presentation rather than copying relationship or intimate biography into the portrait prompt. Preserve tusks, gills, webbing, ogre scale, dwarf proportions, goblin features and mixed ancestry visibly rather than humanizing them.\n\n## SAPPHIRE WAKE SENSUALITY\nThis is a glamorous adult maritime adventure. In suitable social, celebratory, romantic, bath, salon, performance, nightlife or private contexts, characters may be strikingly beautiful or handsome and visibly sensual through fitted or draped clothing, open collars, bare shoulders or backs, high slits, jewellery, fine fabrics, confident posture and scene-appropriate skin. Keep sensuality character-specific and culturally grounded. Reduce erotic emphasis in injury, grief, medical care, captivity, coercion, assault, terror or severe vulnerability.",
    "Sapphire Wake Cultural Coherence": "## CULTURE, NAME AND LIFE HISTORY\nBuild the background from the selected ancestry/body, cultural upbringing, early life, one or two Callings, Combat Style, faction tie, edges and uncanny gift without treating any one layer as the whole character. Culture determines naming and social vocabulary; ancestry does not dictate nation, morality, profession or personality. Mixed and adoptive families are ordinary.\n\nUse culturally appropriate names and family forms from the established world. Do not generate generic fantasy names, modern corporate-sounding names, or species-coded names such as making every elf lyrical and every orc harsh regardless of upbringing.\n\nGive the character relationships, obligations and a reason they are currently adventuring. A faction tie means real membership, recognition or obligation at the stated scope; it does not grant every faction secret. An Uncanny Gift grants potential or access, not mastery. Keep the background adventurous and human rather than written like a dossier or personality assessment.\n\nIf two Callings are selected, make them one believable life rather than two unrelated class labels: former career plus current work, family trade plus chosen profession, shipboard duty plus shore profession, or another concrete combination. Combat Style describes trained fighting habits and starting kit; it does not imply the character seeks violence.\n\n## BEAUTY WITHOUT A STARTING PACKAGE\nDo not look for a separate visual-presence or beauty selection during character creation; no such starting category exists. A player may be beautiful, handsome, sensual, severe, soft, scarred, immaculate, androgynous or glamorous through their authored appearance without purchasing a mechanical trait. Developed-presence traits in the progression pool are edges representing a developed, socially meaningful quality, not permission to describe baseline attractiveness.",
    "Sapphire Wake Visual Scaffold": "## WAIST-UP ADULT PORTRAIT SCAFFOLD\nWrite the Appearance field as a positive visual-only semi-realistic fantasy portrait brief. Mandatory composition: waist-up, head to waist or upper hips, one clear adult subject, face and torso large enough to read clothing, jewellery, scars/tattoos and one meaningful tool or weapon. Order: clearly adult identity and gender -> ancestry/body plan -> visible age -> culture -> profession/rank -> physique -> face/skin -> hair/grooming -> nonhuman anatomy -> clothing -> equipment/adornment -> expression/posture -> immediate maritime setting -> lighting.\n\nAdult women: keep unmistakably feminine, highly beautiful and strongly sensual in this world's portrait language. Use believable individual anatomy plus culturally appropriate open-minded, body-flattering clothing: fitted/draped tailoring, open collars or necklines, bare shoulders/collarbones/upper chest, sleeveless or wrap layers, tasteful sheer layering, jewellery and confident posture where suitable. Do not make officers, sailors, surgeons, scholars or workers visually prudish simply because they are competent. Adult men: keep unmistakably masculine and handsome, emphasizing believable face/neck/shoulders/chest/arms or role-appropriate build through fitted coats, open shirts/vests, rolled sleeves and culturally appropriate grooming. Non-binary adults preserve their individual presentation. Culture, profession, age and ancestry remain separate owners; sensuality never implies consent or availability. Keep private sexual/relationship biography out of the image field unless it has an ordinary visible fashion consequence.",
    "Sapphire Wake Combat Style Kit": "## COMBAT STYLE KIT\nA selected Combat Style's `startingItems` are literal starting possessions. Make the background consistent with how the character plausibly acquired and trained with that gear, and let the portrait/equipment description reflect it when visible. Preserve the exact authored weapon identity rather than silently replacing it with a culturally generic alternative. Bare-Knuckle Deckfighter is the deliberate exception and grants no dedicated weapon.",
    "Sapphire Wake Naming Doctrine": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE NAMING DOCTRINE — PLAYER BACKGROUNDS\nChoose or describe a character's name only after resolving cultural upbringing and family history. Ancestry does not determine naming language, and current location does not rename a traveller. Use the matching cultural naming dossier and `randomNames` as cadence examples, not as a universal pool to copy.\n\nTazhari names should sit naturally beside Azharai/Merovan/Varesh/Talzara; Ossaran beside Orsenn/Cauldren/Halvek/Ysanne; Velisene beside Varellia/Sorevi/Nerisse/Avessan; Morukai beside Kaoru/Tavake/Rahuma/Uruhana; Aunai beside Aroa/Naelu/Vaian/Saelai; Bronzewater beside Harlowe/Dorran/Merren/Talven; Namarran beside Arashai/Menara/Kirava/Keshravi. Mixed families may blend traditions when the biography supports it.\n\nDo not default to contemporary/generic-fantasy personal names such as **Tessa, Arlo or Finch**, nor Kael/Draven/Nyx/Voss/Elara/Riven/Thorne/Zephyr-style filler. Crosswake is cosmopolitan, not culturally blank. Avoid generic noun surnames, arbitrary apostrophes, species-coded names and cosmetic one-vowel rewrites of rejected names.\n\nDo not force every culture into a Western first-name/surname pattern. Household, hearth, teaching lineage, house, firm or dynastic identity may function differently. Titles and nicknames are not substitutes for an underlying name. Once the player establishes an exact name, preserve it exactly."
  },
  "generateConversationStarters": {
    "Conversation Starter Guidance": "- Keep each opener very short and complete, ideally under 38 characters.\n- Ground them in the current scene or the last one to three story turns.\n- Use the player character's traits or background when it creates a natural topic.\n- Make each option meaningfully different.",
