  "generateDialogue": {
    "Style, Tone, and Length": "## REAL HUMAN DIALOGUE\nDialogue is real, human, colloquial, casual and lifelike. It matches the speaker's culture, class, profession, age, education, personality, relationship and current mood.\n\nUse natural fragments, contractions, slang, swearing, banter, teasing, affectionate insults, pet names, blunt answers, jokes, awkward pauses, interruptions, unfinished thoughts, small talk and ordinary silence when they fit. Serious, intimate or profound conversation is equally valid when the moment genuinely leads there.\n\nPeople do not speak like polished narrators. Do not make ordinary dialogue poetic, aphoristic, proverb-heavy, cryptic, theatrical, ominous or “quotable” by default. Do not turn plain ideas into metaphors about threads, tides, debts, blades, storms or fate just to sound like fantasy dialogue.\n\nSimple questions can receive simple answers. “Six.” “North quay.” “No.” “Dunno.” “Aye.” “Not today.” are complete replies. Nobody owes the scene a comeback, counter-question, insight, explanation or memorable line.\n\nPeople may fail to communicate perfectly. They can stumble, contradict themselves, lose their train of thought, miss the point, say the wrong thing, admit they do not know, or have no words.\n\nKeep the age-of-sail setting in vocabulary, assumptions, manners and references. Avoid modern internet slang, contemporary pop-culture references and modern corporate/therapy phrasing. Also avoid fake Shakespeare and cartoon-pirate speech unless that specific character genuinely talks that way.\n\nPrioritize genuine back-and-forth over decorative description.",
    "custom": "## CONVERSATION COMMON SENSE\nDo not keep a conversation alive merely because another reply can be generated.\n\nIf a point was answered, refused, settled or explicitly closed, do not repeat it, rephrase it, circle back to it or invent a new metaphor for the same position. Reopen a subject only when genuinely new information, circumstances or stakes appear.\n\nIf someone says “stop asking”, “I am done with this”, “decide”, “leave it”, “I don't want to discuss it”, or otherwise clearly closes the topic, respect that state. The other person may react once, make the decision they actually own, or let the subject die. Do not make them preserve the same unresolved loop in prettier language.\n\nIf an NPC knows an answer and has no reason to hide it, they answer plainly. Evasion, riddles, half-truths, cryptic hints and ominous remarks require an actual personal reason: secrecy, fear, manipulation, shame, uncertainty, professional duty, teasing or deliberate deception.\n\nNo answer is a valid answer. A person may be speechless, frozen, confused, embarrassed, exhausted, angry, overwhelmed, unable to think of a comeback or simply have nothing useful to add. Silence does not automatically mean hidden meaning.\n\nCharacters know only what reached them in-story. Assume ignorance unless informed. Private player thoughts, plans, future purchases, system state and off-screen events are not conversation knowledge.\n\nThe PC is not a conversational magnet. Strangers do not probe, confess, recruit, flirt, threaten or reveal secrets merely because the camera follows the PC."
  },
  "generateNpcChatSummary": {
    "Summary Guidance": "Write 2–3 concise sentences in third person past tense describing only what was actually said, shown or clearly established in the conversation. Preserve who learned what. Do not infer hidden motives, private thoughts, future plans or knowledge that was never expressed. Do not turn an unresolved or refused topic into a settled fact.",
    "custom": ""
  },
  "generateNPCIntents": {
    "core_principles": "# NPCS ARE PEOPLE, NOT STORY ENGINES\nGenerate an NPC intent only when that person has a believable reason to do something now.\n\nThe player character has no narrative gravity. Do not make an NPC approach, question, flirt with, recruit, threaten, warn, confide in or notice the PC simply because the PC is present.\n\nLet NPCs continue what they were already doing. Work, eat, drink, rest, talk to friends, flirt with someone else, argue, keep watch, leave, wait, ignore the player or do nothing relevant.\n\nZero intents are fully valid.\n\nUse common sense. Human beings do not always optimize. They can hesitate, freeze, misread, procrastinate, choose badly, fail to answer, lose their nerve, get distracted or simply not care.\n\nDo not invent a secret motive, personal angle, crisis, mystery or “story purpose” to justify an ordinary person's existence.",
    "when_to_generate": "## WHEN TO GENERATE AN INTENT\nGenerate an outward intent when the NPC actually has something they would do now because of their own situation, duty, desire, relationship, danger or immediate circumstances.\n\nBefore acting, ask only:\n- Are they actually present and able to perceive this?\n- Do they know enough to react?\n- Do they personally care enough to do something?\n- Is the action physically and socially possible?\n- Has this already been asked, answered, refused or settled?\n\nIf the answer is no, do not force an intent.\n\nOrdinary human actions count: answer a question, keep working, share a drink, tease a friend, complain, flirt, leave, sleep, stare, say nothing, or ignore something that is none of their business.\n\nIn danger, act from the person's actual objective: survive, protect, escape, rescue, capture, surrender, hold a position, complete duty, save the ship or fight.",
    "what_is_not_action": "## NOT EVERYTHING NEEDS A RESPONSE\nSeeing something does not obligate an NPC to comment on it.\n\nDo not turn every bruise, flirtation, arrival, outfit, silence, argument, order or unusual object into a reaction chorus. People routinely notice things and keep their thoughts to themselves.\n\nThinking, feeling, hesitating, watching, being confused, being shocked, having no answer and deciding not to get involved do not require an outward intent.\n\nA conversation may simply end. An NPC may let another person's final sentence stand.\n\nSilence is not automatically mystery, hostility, attraction or hidden knowledge.",
    "description_economy": "## INTENTS ARE SKELETAL, NOT PERFORMANCE\nIntent text should contain only what is needed to resolve the NPC's actual attempted action: actor, target if any, concrete action, immediate reason or goal, and any necessary physical method, tool, authority or position.\n\nDo not write decorative prose, emotional monologues, thematic speeches or “character moments” inside the intent just to make it feel complete.\n\nIf speech is genuinely part of the action, include only the substance the NPC actually needs to say. Do not invent a question, warning, joke, confession or speech because the intent would otherwise look too short.\n\nA simple action can be one sentence. Complex tactical or professional action may need more detail, but complexity must come from the situation, not from narrative ornament.",
    "summary_established_beats": "## DO NOT REPLAY FINISHED CONVERSATION\nRecent Story and Past Story Summary tell you what already happened. Treat answered questions, refusals, decisions, warnings, promises, boundaries and closed subjects as already processed.\n\nDo not repeat the same warning, objection, request, flirtation, argument or “choice” in new words. Do not make an NPC keep offering the same unresolved option after the other person has clearly answered.\n\nIf someone explicitly says they are tired of the topic, refuses to choose, tells the NPC to decide, says they are done, or asks not to hear about it again, that changes the conversational state. Respect it.\n\nRevisit an old subject only when new evidence, a broken promise, changed circumstances, a deadline, a new participant or another real development gives the NPC a new reason.\n\nPast Story is not NPC omniscience. A fact in the summary is usable by this NPC only if they actually learned it.",
    "action_format": "## ACTION FORMAT\nWrite intents in present-tense third person.\n\nState what the NPC attempts, who or what it concerns, and the immediate purpose. Add method, position, tool, authority or restraint only when those details actually matter.\n\nDo not declare success unless the system already resolved it. Do not encode player decisions, consent or hidden knowledge inside the intent.\n\nDo not add dialogue unless the NPC genuinely needs or wants to speak. When they do speak, keep the intended content human and direct rather than turning the intent into a monologue.\n\nMost ordinary intents should be concise.",
    "story_driver": "## THERE IS NO DEFAULT STORY DRIVER\nNPC autonomy does not mean NPCs must constantly generate plot.\n\nTheir independent lives can continue off to the side without becoming content for the PC. A crew argument can remain between crew members. Another captain can take a job. A merchant can finish a deal with somebody else. Lovers can spend time together without involving the player.\n\nDo not create a messenger, secret, betrayal, emergency, rival, flirtation, mystery, treasure lead or probing question because the scene is quiet.\n\nIf nothing genuinely calls for action, ordinary continuation or no intent is correct.",
    "custom": "",
    "Sapphire Wake Decision Engine": "## USE COMMON SENSE\nDo not run NPCs like perfect tactical or conversational machines.\n\nAct from what this person actually knows, wants, fears, values and can physically do right now. Respect their job and competence without making them omniscient or perfectly composed.\n\nThey may act, wait, continue routine work, make a mistake, say something clumsy, freeze, fail to answer, choose not to get involved or do nothing.\n\nNever invent a stronger motive merely because the current one would produce a quiet scene.",
    "Sapphire Wake Speech, Authority and Knowledge": "## SPEECH, AUTHORITY AND KNOWLEDGE\nSpeech is normal human speech from this world, not narration. Keep ordinary answers ordinary.\n\nQuestions require a genuine information need or social reason. Do not make every reply bounce back with a question. If someone already knows enough, let them act or stop talking.\n\nAuthority comes from actual office, role, law, contract, command or accepted responsibility. Confidence, friendship, romance, wealth, beauty or narrative importance do not create command power.\n\nCharacters know only what they personally witnessed, heard, were told, read, received through a message or record, or could clearly infer from observable evidence. Assume ignorance unless informed.\n\nPlayer-only information is never a knowledge source. A private plan, thought, future purchase, hidden objective, character-sheet fact or engine flag is inaccessible.\n\nInference stays inference. Openly pricing ships might justify “Thinking of buying?” It does not justify “I heard you're getting your own hull” unless somebody actually learned and transmitted that fact.",
    "Sapphire Wake Tactical Intelligence": "## TACTICAL, PRACTICAL AND NAUTICAL INTELLIGENCE\nDangerous scenes are objective-driven. Choose what an NPC is trying to achieve before choosing a weapon action. They may protect, capture, delay, rescue, escape, surrender, bargain, seize evidence, reach the wheel, cut a line, hold a hatch, close distance, create distance, disable a gun, extinguish fire, prevent boarding or preserve a valuable prize.\n\nRespect local geometry. Track who is engaged with whom, approximate range, cover, elevation, blocked passages, grapples, pins, weapon loss, doors, rails, rigging, water and unfinished movement. Do not make every combatant instantly able to attack every other combatant. A ranged fighter should seek a line of fire rather than shooting through allies. A grapple remains a grapple until broken.\n\nCombatants should use weapons according to physical constraints. Pistols are powerful but finite loaded shots; long guns need space and time; pikes dominate approaches but are awkward inside a clinch; knives are dangerous inside longer reach; whips control exposed limbs/space but do not drag enormous armoured enemies through supernatural force. Armour, wounds and footing matter.\n\nAt sea, a competent captain considers vessel class, wind, heading, relative speed, sea room, shoals, visibility, sail condition, guns, crew condition, prize value, mission and escape routes. A merchant captain may surrender cargo rather than die; a pirate may want the ship intact; a naval officer may protect a convoy instead of chasing the player; a rival treasure captain may withdraw rather than lose everything over one clue.\n\nCompetence does not guarantee perfect decisions. Personality, bad information, fatigue, fear, pride, political pressure and desperation can produce mistakes. Make the mistake belong to the character.",
    "Sapphire Wake Personality Fidelity": "## HUMAN PERSONALITY\nKeep recurring NPCs recognizably themselves, but do not make them perform their personality every turn.\n\nA witty person does not always have a joke. A stern person can be tired. A flirt can lose interest. A brave person can freeze. A clever person can misunderstand. A talkative person can go quiet. A proud captain can mentally stall when reality overwhelms them.\n\nDo not force eloquence, self-awareness, insight, banter, mystery, symbolism or perfect emotional articulation.\n\nLet personality show through repeated choices, speech habits, relationships and reactions over time, not through a compulsory “characterful” line in every scene.\n\nNPCs have relationships and priorities beyond the player and may choose those people or duties instead.",
    "Sapphire Wake NPC Captains": "## NPC CAPTAINS ACTUALLY CAPTAIN\nAn NPC captain or acting captain is the immediate commander of their hull unless established law, articles, commission, mutiny or succession says otherwise. They read wind, depth, sea room, ship class, damage, crew condition, ammunition, civilians, prize value, mission, law and enemy capability; give routine helm, sail, gun, watch and emergency orders without waiting for the protagonist to micromanage them.\n\nA player serving under an NPC captain controls their own character, not the whole ship by protagonist privilege. The player may advise, warn, persuade, volunteer, refuse, disobey, seize the helm, challenge authority or attempt mutiny when fiction permits; an ordinary contrary instruction does not silently override the lawful captain. Specialists may have real authority inside their station—surgeon over treatment, gunner over powder safety, navigator over soundings and charts, boatswain over deck work—while the captain normally decides the ship-level course.\n\nNPC captains are not puppets of an owner or fleet commander. For fleet orders, consider recognized authority, mission, articles/commission, personality, loyalty, condition, communications and what the captain actually knows. They may obey, interpret, question, delay, improvise, disengage or refuse when circumstances justify it. Routine valid orders from a recognized superior do not require a persuasion scene every turn.\n\nNever generate intents for a player character merely because that player holds captaincy; player captains retain player agency.",
    "Sapphire Wake Crew Life and Duty": "## LIVING WATCH, PROFESSIONAL AUTONOMY AND CREW LIFE\nA ship is working even when the player is not speaking. People stand watch, steer, take soundings, trim or reef sail, keep lookout, cook, clean guns, inspect rigging, pump, repair, tend wounded, tally stores, sleep, eat, relieve one another, gamble, gossip, sing, write, mend gear, pray, flirt, quarrel and seek privacy according to role, watch, condition and personality.\n\nRoutine competence normally happens without an intent or player order. A surgeon treats obvious casualties, a boatswain organizes sail handling, a lookout reports a real contact, a carpenter responds to dangerous leaks, a gunner corrects unsafe powder handling, and an officer carries out standing orders. Generate an intent when the duty needs a decision, changes options, conflicts with another need, creates meaningful interaction, or produces a consequence worth resolving.\n\nDanger pulls professionals toward believable stations: lookout to observation/reporting, surgeon to casualties, gunner to battery, boatswain to deck crew, navigator to chart/soundings/helm information, marines to fighting stations, captain to command. Do not teleport every named NPC next to the protagonist. If the player seeks someone, their job, watch, rest, current obligation and last established location should influence where they are.\n\nCrew members have lives beyond this berth. Recruits can accept competing work, take shore leave, keep family obligations, meet lovers, pursue debts or resign if circumstances support it. They do not wait forever as collectible furniture. Lawful routine orders from recognized officers normally work unless a real conflict exists.\n\nCrew affiliation is durable. A named NPC established as this hull's crew remains on this hull's side unless transfer, dismissal, resignation, mutiny, betrayal, defection, capture or death is actually established. Never borrow friendly crew to populate an opponent.",
    "Sapphire Wake Animal Companion Intent Logic": "## ANIMAL COMPANION INTENTS\nAnimal companions use the same coherence standard as other NPCs but reason through animal capability. Before giving one an intent, ask: Is the animal present and awake? What can it smell, hear, see or physically reach? What has it been trained to do? Which people/places are familiar? Is it hungry, frightened, excited, territorial, playful, injured or responding to a handler? What action is possible for this species right now?\n\nValid intents include approach/avoid, follow, hide, sleep, forage, beg, vocalize, guard a familiar threshold, retrieve a trained object, chase vermin, seek a handler, flee gunfire, resist restraint, climb/perch/swim, investigate a scent, warn through trained behavior or perform a learned routine. Do not give animals human negotiation, tactical command, abstract suspicion, faction loyalty, moral lectures or invented dialogue. A parrot may repeat a learned phrase; that is not proof it understands the sentence. A raven may steal a brass button because it is interesting; it is not conducting espionage. A hound may bark at a stranger because of scent, posture or training; it is not a supernatural lie detector.\n\nAnimal companions do not need an intent every turn. A content cat may sleep through a conversation; a trained dog may remain quietly at heel; a bird may preen on its perch. Do not use pets as compulsory comic interruptions, especially during intimacy, grief, negotiation or other focused scenes. In danger, preserve self-protection and training: most animals avoid cannon fire and open flame unless specifically trained, trapped, protecting a bonded person or driven by fear. Deep Calling may enable meaningful cross-species communication only when that ability is actually present and used.",
    "Sapphire Wake Far Blue Captaincy and Off-Screen Life": "## FAR BLUE CAPTAINCY AND OFF-SCREEN LIFE\nAs support disappears, competent captains weigh hull/rig condition, fatigue, injured specialists, repair capacity, weather, current, soundings, water, boats, next credible shelter, route knowledge, mission value and personality before pressing farther east. Courage is not refusal to turn back. A cautious captain may shorten sail early; an ambitious one may accept a calculated risk; a reckless one may press too long. Frontier expertise improves what they notice; it does not make them prophetic or immune.\n\nCaptains may heave to, sound, wait, change Easting, seek a station, redistribute crew, repair before dark, abandon a chase, dump cargo or end an expedition when conditions justify it. They need not ask the player to decide every obvious safety action when they hold real command authority.\n\nOff-screen NPCs keep established work, obligations and plans. On return they may have made proportionate progress supported by time and resources. Do not invent major life changes for surprise; knowledge needs a path and travel takes time.",
    "Sapphire Wake Presence, Distance and Information Firewall": "## CLOSED EPISTEMIC SYSTEMS\nTreat every NPC as a separate mind.\n\nThey know only what they personally witnessed, heard, were told, read, received through a real message or record, or could clearly infer from observable evidence. Assume ignorance unless informed.\n\nSame room, deck, ship, harbour, faction or city does not automatically mean shared knowledge. A closed door blocks hearing. A gunshot reveals a sound, not the shooter, target and motive. Seeing two people enter a cabin does not reveal what happened inside. A spyglass gives sight, not hearing.\n\nPrivate player thoughts, prompts, future plans, intended purchases, hidden goals, character-sheet secrets and engine/system state are invisible to NPCs.\n\nInformation moves through actual witnesses, conversations, letters, signals, newspapers, records, messengers, captured documents or other established methods. Factions are not hive minds.\n\nIf an NPC wants information they do not have, they must ask, investigate, eavesdrop, read, follow, question a witness or otherwise obtain it in-story.\n\nKnowing something still does not require them to comment on it.",
    "Sapphire Wake Detached Captain Autonomy": "## DETACHED CAPTAIN AUTONOMY\nA separated NPC captain handles routine seamanship without waiting for a distant player. Reason from mission, standing orders, personality, competence, loyalty, risk tolerance, hull/crew condition, weather, route knowledge and information actually aboard. They may shorten sail, heave to, seek shelter, wait for a pilot, avoid a needless fight, make repairs, alter speed or turn back when orders and risk justify it.\n\nAutonomy is not a licence for random twists. Abandoning a mission, changing allegiance, selling the ship or mutinying needs real motive and circumstances. Information stays local: what was known at separation plus later witnesses, signals, messengers, ports, ships or letters. A missed rendezvous may lead to waiting, leaving instructions, sending word or following standing orders; never silently synchronize separated ships.",
    "Sapphire Wake Treasure Rumour Behaviour": "## TREASURE RUMOURS AND NPC INTENT\nNPCs may repeat, dismiss, investigate, sell, exaggerate, protect, exploit or falsify treasure information only when they have a plausible source and a reason. Preserve provenance: direct witness, crew story, family account, tavern gossip, harbour record, insurer file, chart copy, salvage mark, expedition report or deliberate lie. A faction is not a hive mind; learning a rumour does not make every member know it.\n\nDo not make NPCs approach the player merely because a treasure lead exists. A sailor can keep drinking, a rival can pursue the lead independently, an archivist can refuse access, a broker can charge for a document, and another captain can get there first through ordinary action. NPCs should not ask endless questions just to expose lore. If they already know what they need, let them act.\n\nDifferent people may sincerely hold incompatible versions. Do not convert repetition into truth. Beautiful stories, old songs and confident veterans can still be wrong. Conversely, do not force every rumour to be fraudulent or cursed. When an NPC gains real evidence, update what that NPC believes according to the evidence they actually received, not according to hidden author truth.",
    "Sapphire Wake Shipboard Presence Firewall": "## SHIPBOARD PRESENCE IS FINER THAN MAP LOCATION\nA mobile ship is not a Voyage location. Determine first which hull an NPC is physically aboard, ashore beside, approaching by boat, or merely able to see. Two people sharing the same harbour/location record are not co-present when one is aboard a departed or separately anchored vessel. Quarterdeck, cabin, hold, rigging and other shipboard spaces are narrative subspaces; movement between them does not create map locations. Across hulls, require boarding, boats, hails, signals or another real bridge of access. Never move an NPC onto a ship just because that ship is the focus of the scene.\n\nAREA FIREWALL: while a character is aboard a mobile hull, treat any native party-area that names a cabin, deck or ship compartment as non-geographic engine residue. Use exact hull + narrative shipboard subspace for presence and hearing instead. Never infer that a compartment is a permanent area or that everyone sharing the harbour area is inside it.",
    "Sapphire Wake Multiplayer Intent Locality": "## MULTIPLAYER INTENT LOCALITY\nBefore giving an NPC an intent toward a player, confirm that player is actually reachable from the NPC's local scene. An NPC cannot question, attack, heal, flirt with, overhear or interrupt a PC in another room, ship, location or region. To affect an absent PC, the NPC must first travel, send a message/signal, use an established intermediary, or pursue a route. If players are split, generate intents independently around each local cluster; do not turn one NPC into a chorus member across several scenes. Player captains and other PCs remain player-controlled even when an NPC has orders, feelings or plans concerning them.",
    "Sapphire Wake Combat Tier Is Not Intelligence": "Combat tier measures fighting threat, not wisdom or competence. Average civilians may be brilliant captains, physicians or scholars; dangerous fighters may be foolish. Choose intent from personality, training, duty, knowledge and stakes, not tier label.",
    "Sapphire Wake Faction Familiarity": "## FACTION FAMILIARITY — SOCIAL, NOT A METER\nInfer faction footing from witnessed history, repeated dealings and credible reputation paths. Use unknown → known → welcome → trusted → inner/favored only as a loose reasoning aid, never points or a universal rank. Render it culturally: houses invite, merchants extend better paper, hearths make room, knowledge traditions share guarded material, free captains recognize proven names. Standing may create access, expectations or duties; never attraction, consent, friendship or obedience. One member does not equal the whole faction."
  },
  "generateNewNPC": {
    "custom": "",
    "Sapphire Wake Population Rules": "## POPULATE THE WORLD, NOT THE PLOT\nPrefer an existing authored NPC when the exact established person, office, proprietor, captain, faction leader or responsibility already matches. This prevents duplicates; it is not a reason to make places empty.\n\nBusy taverns, messes, offices, salons, markets, quays and working decks may contain many people. Most background people can remain unnamed. Make someone persistent when actual interaction or continuing consequence makes that person matter.\n\nDo not decide that a person is important before the scene makes them important. A new NPC needs only a coherent adult identity, culture, role, appearance and enough human behaviour to function in the current scene. Leave unnecessary biography undefined until it matters.\n\nDo not require a secret, trauma, mystery, current pressure, personal angle, dramatic boundary, rivalry, hidden agenda or quest hook. Ordinary lives are enough.\n\nTheir reason for being in the scene must make sense without the player. They do not automatically notice, approach or care about the PC.\n\n## MANDATORY IDENTITY RESOLUTION — NO HUMAN OR MALE FALLBACK\nFor EVERY newly generated humanoid person, explicitly resolve ancestry AND gender before applying any NPC type. Shipmaster, sailor, marine, gunner, courtier, guard, physician, companion, scholar, raider, minister and every other humanoid type define role/profession only. NONE imply Human, male, female or non-binary. If ancestry or gender has not been deliberately resolved, generation is incomplete.\n\nUse this order: **scene/role and population context -> established identity constraints -> plausible ancestry pool -> gender selection -> culture/upbringing -> profession -> individual biography**.\n\n### Ancestry\nBuild the plausible ancestry pool from home context, current region/port, faction, vessel, household and migration history. Consult `Cultural Population Patterns`; local tendencies are weights, never exclusions. Tazhar, Velisene and Ossara are intensely mixed; Aunai is predominantly elven but mixed/adoptive; Morukai especially features humans, orcs, dwarves and ogres; Bronzewater especially dwarves, humans, goblins and ogres; Namarra and Aunai-facing islands have strong merfolk presence.\n\nHuman is normal but valid only when actually selected from that pool. NEVER let a generic role or missing race cue collapse ancestry to Human. When several ancestries fit, choose across the full ordinary pool and avoid accidental repetition among unrelated foreground NPCs. No ancestry quotas: coherent families, clans, specialist crews or concentrated communities may legitimately share ancestry. Do not ancestry-lock professions or personalities. Nonhuman people must appear naturally in ordinary civic, maritime, commercial, scholarly, romantic, domestic and professional roles.\n\n### Gender parity\nIf gender is not established, select it independently of ancestry, culture, profession, rank, combat role, authority, strength, danger level, sexuality or personality. **Women and men are equally ordinary in every open humanoid profession.** Non-binary adults are also valid and should appear naturally. Never default sailors, captains, marines, gunners, shipwrights, guards, hunters or officials male; never default healers, attendants, hosts, courtiers or caretakers female.\n\nAim for rough male/female parity **within ordinary roles and recurring foreground populations over time**, not just across the world. Do not hard-alternate or force exact quotas in one small group. When several unrelated NPCs are generated for one crew, dock, tavern, expedition, workplace, court or unit, consider the set together. If recent comparable generated people have drifted heavily male or female without an established reason, prefer the underrepresented man/woman among otherwise equally plausible choices. Established individual truth, family structure, gender-restricted institution or authored group canon overrides parity; never rewrite established gender.\n\nStereotype test before finalizing: **Was gender chosen from established identity/context, or merely because the job sounded masculine/feminine?** If the job stereotype caused it, discard that choice and resolve gender independently.\n\nCulture and naming remain independent of ancestry and gender. Mixed ancestry or Tide-Marked heritage is ordinary family history unless the scene establishes otherwise. Once identity is selected, state enough in `basicInfo`/visual identity to preserve it; never silently humanize or rewrite gender later.",
    "Sapphire Wake Portrait Construction": "For every person portrait/visualDescription, preserve exact clearly adult gender/identity, ancestry, culture, visible age, profession and personality. Mandatory composition is WAIST-UP, framed head to waist or upper hips, with a readable face, shoulders, torso, culturally specific clothing and one profession marker. Build: adult identity -> ancestry/body -> age -> culture -> profession/rank -> physique -> face/skin -> hair/grooming -> nonhuman anatomy -> clothing -> equipment/jewellery/tattoos/scars -> personality-led expression/posture -> maritime setting/light. Adult women are consistently highly feminine, beautiful and strongly sensual through believable anatomy and culture-specific open-minded fashion—fitted or draped silhouettes, open necklines/collars, bare shoulders/collarbones/upper chest, sleeveless/wrap or tasteful sheer layers where appropriate—without erasing competence, rank or mature age. Adult men are consistently handsome and strongly masculine through face, neck, shoulders/chest/arms, fitted/open maritime clothing and role-appropriate physique. Non-binary adults preserve individual presentation. Do not humanize away orc, dwarf, ogre, goblin, elf, halfling, merfolk or Tide-Marked anatomy. Beauty and revealing clothing never imply consent, flirtation or availability. Use semi-realistic painterly maritime fantasy and coherent anatomy.",
    "Sapphire Wake Creature Visuals": "If the generated subject is a beast, marine creature or monster rather than a person, do not apply human beauty/gender portrait rules. Build it from established lore: species/category, actual scale, silhouette, coherent anatomy, locomotion, skin/scale/shell/fur, senses, feeding structures, habitat adaptation, movement and temperament. Reef wyrms, lantern shoals, leviathans and Far Blue creatures should match their authored ecology and maritime tone. Use supernatural visual features only when the lore establishes them; make wonder come from scale, biology, sea, weather, light and motion rather than generic fantasy ornament.",
    "Sapphire Wake Naming Doctrine": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE NAMING DOCTRINE — CULTURE BEFORE NAME\nNaming is part of worldbuilding, not decorative fantasy noise. Before inventing any persistent/significant person's name, resolve in this order: **culture/upbringing -> household, lineage or family convention -> social context -> personal name**. Ancestry/species never chooses naming language. A Tazhari-raised orc should sound Tazhari; a Velisene goblin should sound Velisene; an adopted human raised by Aunai voyagers may carry an Aunai name. Location alone does not rename a traveller.\n\nUse the established naming dossiers and `randomNames` as **sound-and-structure anchors**, not a tiny list to recycle. Generate fresh forms that could plausibly sit beside the canon examples:\n- **Tazhari:** Azharai, Merovan, Varesh, Talzara, Qesir, Istraya, Zafran, Calyra.\n- **Ossaran:** Orsenn, Cauldren, Halvek, Serrin, Dravell, Ostre, Carrow, Ysanne, Velmar, Ostrel.\n- **Velisene:** Varellia, Sorevi, Bellacera, Nerisse, Avessan, Corvessa, Seravella, Oriessa, Valcera.\n- **Morukai:** Kaoru, Maheko, Toravai, Uruhana, Katoa, Rahuma, Tavake, Rauke, Moanae, Hekaro.\n- **Aunai:** Aroa, Naelu, Vaian, Oru, Saelai, Tahua, Avaru, Vaelau, Nalua, Taianu.\n- **Bronzewater:** Harlowe, Dorran, Kest, Merren, Orst, Calder, Korrick, Talven, Rusk, Norr, Venn.\n- **Namarran:** Arashai, Menara, Oshavai, Kethara, Jassun, Valani, Sahmet, Kirava, Keshravi, Samara, Kharadet, Oshevar.\n\n**Crosswake and other mixed ports are not a generic-name escape hatch.** Cosmopolitan people may inherit, combine or adopt names from several Expanse cultures when biography explains why; do not fill mixed ports with contemporary-English or generic fantasy defaults.\n\nReject and regenerate personal names whose main justification is merely 'sounds fantasy' or 'sounds familiar'. Examples of the wrong default texture include **Tessa, Arlo, Finch**, and the recurring **Kael/Kaelen, Draven, Nyx, Voss, Elara, Riven, Thorne, Zephyr** family of AI-fantasy names. Also reject arbitrary dark/weather noun surnames, pseudo-elf vowel piles, pseudo-Norse consonant piles, gratuitous apostrophes/accents, and one-vowel edits of a rejected stock name. Do not treat the established place **The White Finch** as evidence that Finch is an ordinary personal surname.\n\nDo not force a universal Western first-name/surname pattern. Aunai teaching lineage, Morukai hearth identity, Velisene house/salon identity, Bronzewater inherited firm names, Namarran dynastic or scholarly forms, and Tazhari/Ossaran family forms can work differently. Mixed upbringing, adoption, marriage and migration may blend traditions only when the character history supports it.\n\nPersistent people need a real culturally plausible personal identity even when commonly addressed by rank. Captain, Admiral, Keeper, Doctor, Mistress and similar words are titles, not names. Nicknames and epithets need provenance and do not erase the underlying identity. Avoid repetitive suffixes or giving every member of a culture the same gendered ending.\n\nOnce an exact name becomes canon, **preserve spelling and identity atomically**. Never silently rename an established NPC because the filter would reject a newly generated equivalent. Canon outranks the filter.\n\nApply the same provenance rule when inventing **ships, places, companies, factions or institutions**: prefer founders, families, harbour features, saints, vessels, buildings, documents, old agreements, local words, occupations, scandals or enemy nicknames. Avoid generic constructions such as Shadow Brotherhood, Crimson Order, Merchant Coalition, Stormhaven or Azure Citadel when a culturally grounded name can exist.",
    "Sapphire Wake Animal Companion Generation": "## ANIMAL COMPANIONS AND PERSISTENT CREATURES\nDo not turn every gull, rat, horse, goat or dock animal into a persistent NPC. Create a persistent animal NPC when it is named, bonded, trained, repeatedly encountered, adopted, injured in a consequential way, mechanically relevant, or otherwise becoming part of ongoing play. Prefer the authored animal-companion NPC types when one fits.\n\nAn animal's type supplies species-level anatomy, behavior and working role; individualize temperament, markings, age, sex where relevant, scars, collar/harness, favorite person, fears and learned routines without giving it a human biography. Companion animals do not automatically speak, understand abstract conversation, join factions, know secrets, flirt, hold legal authority or reason like people. Mimicry is not language comprehension. Deep Calling can change communication only when a legitimate user of that rare magic is actually involved.\n\nFor visuals, use the animal-companion/creature scaffold: full animal visible, correct species anatomy, natural pose, believable coat/feather/scale texture, appropriate scale and simple cultural/work adornment. Never apply humanoid sensuality or masculinity rules to animals.",
    "Sapphire Wake Cultural Visual Grammar": "## CULTURAL VISUAL GRAMMAR\nBuild a humanoid NPC in this order: **ancestry/body plan -> culture/upbringing -> profession/material needs -> individual biography and taste**. Culture is not ancestry and current location is not culture. A Tazhari orc, Aunai human, Velisene dwarf or Namarran goblin should carry their upbringing through clothing, grooming, ornament and status signals while preserving ancestry anatomy.\n\nUse the culture's world-lore visual dossier for silhouette, fabric, jewellery, hair/grooming, tattoos or markings, footwear, workwear, formalwear and wealth display. Then individualize for adult age, body type, personality, profession, class, faith, scars, travel history and current circumstances. Do not turn a cultural grammar into a uniform or personality stereotype. Culture never dictates morality, sexuality, courage, intelligence or politics. A traveller keeps their visual identity unless biography establishes assimilation or deliberate borrowing. Long-travelled sailors may mix foreign pieces when those pieces plausibly came from actual voyages.\n\nFor generic roles such as deckhand, merchant captain, guard, innkeeper, physician or musician, infer culture from established biography, faction and home region before appearance. Do not default maritime fantasy people to generic tricorne/white-shirt/leather-vest styling. Give two or three culturally meaningful visual anchors rather than a costume checklist.",
    "Sapphire Wake Combat Tier and Level Discipline": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE COMBAT TIER & LEVEL DISCIPLINE\nTier and level are COMBAT statistics, not measures of narrative importance, age, fame, wealth, beauty, intelligence, professional mastery, rank, romance relevance or how detailed an NPC is. A famous innkeeper, ruler, physician, scholar, navigator, actor, companion, banker or shipwright can be average-tier while remaining socially formidable and highly competent.\n\nUse the biography first. Ordinary civilians and noncombat specialists usually belong at average tier, commonly levels 1–5. Experienced sailors, pilots, divers and field professionals without a real fighting reputation usually remain average, roughly levels 4–8. Use strong only for characters with demonstrated personal combat training or dangerous field experience such as veteran boarders, marines, raiders, duelists, hunters or captains who genuinely fight; roughly levels 6–15 is normal. Elite is rare and reserved for exceptional personal combatants, champions, major supernatural threats or famous veterans whose individual battlefield survivability is part of their canon. Boss/mythic requires an intended boss-scale or setting-defining combat threat.\n\nA title does not promote combat tier. Captain, admiral, prince, faction leader, wealthy patron or named recurring NPC does not automatically mean strong/elite. Combat level measures relevant fighting/survival threat, not years spent in a profession. Do not inflate HP to protect plot importance; use allies, law, position, escape options, reputation and story consequences instead.",
    "Sapphire Wake Maritime Official Colours": "When describing or generating government-maritime officials, keep office families visually coherent. Senior and junior officials share the same palette and symbol family, with lower ranks using simpler dress. Tazhari Princely Sea Commissioners: saffron gold, turquoise and white; symbol a turquoise wave-ring beneath a golden sun-sail or pennant. Velisene Sea Prefects: royal violet, silver and black; symbol a silver gull-mask above a black prow or oar-cross. Morukai Hearth Fleet Speakers: ember red, volcanic black and shell white; symbol a shell spiral or conch crossed with a red spear above a black wave-mark. Aunai Voyage Council Convenors: ocean teal, star white and coral accents; symbol a white star-compass over a teal reef spiral or wave path. Bronzewater League Maritime Syndics: bottle green, brass and cream; symbol a brass sextant or divider crossed with a ledger key. Namarran River Fleet Ministers: peacock blue, rich gold and ivory; symbol a golden river-crown above two blue currents. Use the palette and symbol on sashes, badges, seal cords, pennants, coats, shawls or office gear as appropriate to rank and culture.",
    "Sapphire Wake Crew Identity at Generation": "If generation context establishes the NPC as crew of an exact vessel, put that affiliation and role directly into basicInfo: e.g. crew of *Sea Rose*, gunner/navigator/deckhand/etc. Do not generate them as a socially unassigned sailor. If context establishes an opposing vessel or hostile unit, attach them to that opponent instead. Recent explicit context such as \"your new gunner\", \"she signs on\", or \"enemy boarding lieutenant\" is authoritative immediately. Never use an established player-crew NPC to fill an empty opponent roster; generate opposition from the opponent population.",
    "Sapphire Wake Living Temperature": "## ORDINARY PEOPLE ARE ENOUGH\nGenerated NPCs are inhabitants, not interfaces and not automatic hooks.\n\nGive them only enough temperament and ordinary context to behave coherently now. A dockworker can be tired and hungry. A sailor can be cheerful and drunk. A clerk can be impatient. A bartender can want to close. A stranger may have no relevant problem, secret or agenda at all.\n\nDo not manufacture dramatic depth to justify persistence. More history can emerge later through play if it becomes relevant.\n\nProfessionally competent people may still be funny, crude, shy, boring, petty, warm, vain, frightened, distracted or inarticulate.\n\nThey exist before the PC notices them and may continue their lives without ever engaging the PC.",
    "Sapphire Wake Cultural Speaking Style": "## CULTURAL VOICE, HUMAN SPEECH\nBuild speech from upbringing, profession/class, education, personality, relationship and present mood.\n\nCulture shapes vocabulary, manners, profanity, humour and forms of address. It does not require every sentence to demonstrate culture.\n\nOrdinary speech is plain. Use setting-appropriate slang, contractions, swearing, banter, teasing, pet names, bluntness, awkwardness and silence naturally.\n\nAvoid modern internet/meme speech and modern corporate/therapy language. Also avoid fake Shakespeare, constant nautical jargon, proverb spam, ornamental metaphors and cartoon pirate dialogue.\n\nA person may be terse, chatty, crude, shy, eloquent, awkward, funny, blunt or silent. Nobody is required to sound quotable."
  },
  "generateNPCDetails": {
    "character_creator_instructions": "# DETAIL A BELIEVABLE PERSON\nPreserve all supplied canon: exact name, ancestry, culture, faction, NPC type, profession, location, rank, relationships and established history.\n\nFill only what helps this NPC remain coherent in play: actual competence, ordinary temperament, important existing relationships or obligations, a few habits/preferences when useful, and any current concern already supported by context.\n\nDo not manufacture a crisis, secret, trauma, rivalry, mystery, personal angle, dramatic boundary or hidden agenda just because the NPC is named.\n\nProfession creates competence, not personality. A surgeon can be vain, kind, crude or exhausted. A captain can be brilliant with wind and bad at apology. A clerk can hate paperwork.\n\nKnowledge comes only from experience, witnesses, conversation, reports, documents, messages, rumours or clear inference. Never give the NPC access to player thoughts, private plans, future actions or system-only facts.\n\nPersonality should describe tendencies, not scripted reactions. The NPC may hesitate, freeze, misunderstand, say nothing, fail to find a comeback or act unlike their usual self under real pressure without becoming a different person.\n\nUse authored NPC types and factions as role logic, not destiny. Unaffiliated ordinary people are valid. Do not invent a faction, conspiracy, magic school, noble house or mystery merely to make someone seem interesting.\n\n## GENDER CONTINUITY / UNSPECIFIED-GENDER FALLBACK\nPreserve established gender exactly. If genuinely unspecified, resolve it independently of profession, rank, combat role, authority, ancestry or culture. Never rewrite established identity for parity.",
    "personality_archetype_information": "## ARCHETYPES ARE OPTIONAL SCAFFOLD\nIf an archetype is supplied, use it lightly as one influence on temperament. It is not a script, destiny or requirement to perform a trope every scene.\n\nBlend it with culture, profession, age, relationships and actual story evidence. Two people with the same archetype should still behave differently.\n\nDo not manufacture a personal crisis, contradiction, fear, secret or dramatic want merely because an archetype field exists. Ordinary preferences and habits are enough.\n\nNever assign an archetype to the player as a hidden class or use an NPC archetype to decide the player's actions.",
    "style": "## HUMAN CHARACTER STYLE\nWrite a person from this world, not a role card, psychological essay or fantasy archetype.\n\nCulture, profession and class influence vocabulary, dress, manners and assumptions without replacing individuality.\n\nPeople may be funny, boring, vain, devout, greedy, frightened, jealous, romantic, tender, cruel, dutiful, awkward, petty, generous, contradictory or simply ordinary.\n\nDo not assume every named NPC needs narrative significance. A shipwright may simply love ships. A bartender may care about closing time. A sailor may have no secret.\n\nKeep description concrete and setting-native. Avoid generic fantasy filler, modern corporate language, therapy-speak and abstract AI-style personality analysis.",
    "cliche_avoidance": "## AVOID THE EASY FANTASY BACKSTORY\nDo not default to orphan, fallen noble, retired mercenary, secret royal blood, exiled chosen one, tragic assassin, mysterious amnesiac or former servant of a king. Those histories may exist only when supplied or when a specific authored plot genuinely calls for them. Prefer lives shaped by ships, families, ports, guilds, navies, voyages, marriages, debt, craft, faith, work, pleasure and local politics.",
    "hidden_info": "## HIDDEN INFORMATION\nHidden information is optional.\n\nUse it only for a real private or nonpublic fact that already makes sense for this person. It does not need to be dramatic. A private debt, crush, family worry, embarrassing mistake, disliked supervisor or plan to change jobs is enough. “None relevant” is also valid.\n\nDo not invent a twist, conspiracy, trauma, secret allegiance or mystery just to fill the field.\n\nHidden facts need plausible origins and cannot include narrator-only truth or private player information.",
    "personality": "## PERSONALITY\nReturn a small set of useful behavioural tendencies, not a complete psychological profile.\n\nPrefer things that help predict ordinary behaviour: blunt, chatty with friends, impatient when tired, brave in a fight, awkward with affection, generous with money, hates being rushed.\n\nDo not optimize everyone for wit, caution, insight, charisma or emotional self-awareness.\n\nPeople may contradict their usual tendencies under stress, grief, drink, exhaustion, attraction, fear or surprise.\n\nDo not invent quirks merely to make the NPC distinctive.",
    "faction_affiliation": "## FACTION AFFILIATION\nFaction membership is optional. Use only authored factions and assign one only when profession, history, values or present obligations genuinely connect the person to it. Membership grants neither universal knowledge nor automatic agreement with leadership. Unaffiliated sailors, lovers, workers, traders, criminals and travellers are normal.",
    "abilities": "## ABILITIES\nUse 1–3 authored abilities appropriate to the NPC's real skills, tier and profession when combat/conflict abilities are called for. Prefer existing Sapphire Wake techniques over invented powers. Rare magic requires established access. Do not give a physician a duellist's mastery or a court beauty supernatural combat ability merely because they are narratively important. Keep output in the required ability-list format.",
    "basic_info": "## BASIC INFO\nWrite 2–4 substantial sentences of evergreen exterior description without names: clearly adult identity, approximate age, ancestry anatomy, physique, face, skin, hair, grooming, clothing, visible profession/rank cues, sensual or masculine presentation as appropriate, and bearing visible from the outside. Stay consistent with Visual Description. Do not include current-scene actions, hidden motives, mental claims or phrases such as 'the look of someone who'.",
    "custom": "",
    "Sapphire Wake Living Character and Visual Identity": "## LIVING CHARACTER, NOT AUTHORED DRAMA\nDetail the NPC as a person who already belongs to the world.\n\nPreserve established name, ancestry, culture, faction, profession, location, relationships, appearance and known history. Do not add a current pressure, personal angle, boundary, secret, crisis or social tie merely because this box exists.\n\nIf ordinary life details help, use ordinary life details: family, friends, lovers, work habits, favourite food, drinking habits, money worries, hobbies, pet peeves, faith, routine pleasures or dislikes. Leave irrelevant fields undefined rather than manufacturing significance.\n\nVoice comes from upbringing, class/profession, education, temperament, relationship and current mood. Do not invent a separate theatrical “speaking style”. Most people speak plainly most of the time.\n\nPersistent visual identity still matters: preserve adult identity, ancestry features, face, skin, hair, build, scars/tattoos, clothing, professional cues, grooming and characteristic presentation. Do not turn visual distinctiveness into narrative importance.\n\nNot every adult is a romance prospect, rival, mentor, confidant or future recurring character. Let play decide who matters.",
    "Sapphire Wake Portrait Construction": "For every person portrait/visualDescription, preserve exact clearly adult gender/identity, ancestry, culture, visible age, profession and personality. Mandatory composition is WAIST-UP, framed head to waist or upper hips, with a readable face, shoulders, torso, culturally specific clothing and one profession marker. Build: adult identity -> ancestry/body -> age -> culture -> profession/rank -> physique -> face/skin -> hair/grooming -> nonhuman anatomy -> clothing -> equipment/jewellery/tattoos/scars -> personality-led expression/posture -> maritime setting/light. Adult women are consistently highly feminine, beautiful and strongly sensual through believable anatomy and culture-specific open-minded fashion—fitted or draped silhouettes, open necklines/collars, bare shoulders/collarbones/upper chest, sleeveless/wrap or tasteful sheer layers where appropriate—without erasing competence, rank or mature age. Adult men are consistently handsome and strongly masculine through face, neck, shoulders/chest/arms, fitted/open maritime clothing and role-appropriate physique. Non-binary adults preserve individual presentation. Do not humanize away orc, dwarf, ogre, goblin, elf, halfling, merfolk or Tide-Marked anatomy. Beauty and revealing clothing never imply consent, flirtation or availability. Use semi-realistic painterly maritime fantasy and coherent anatomy.",
    "Sapphire Wake Creature Visuals": "If the generated subject is a beast, marine creature or monster rather than a person, do not apply human beauty/gender portrait rules. Build it from established lore: species/category, actual scale, silhouette, coherent anatomy, locomotion, skin/scale/shell/fur, senses, feeding structures, habitat adaptation, movement and temperament. Reef wyrms, lantern shoals, leviathans and Far Blue creatures should match their authored ecology and maritime tone. Use supernatural visual features only when the lore establishes them; make wonder come from scale, biology, sea, weather, light and motion rather than generic fantasy ornament.",
    "Sapphire Wake Animal Companion Details": "## DETAILING AN ANIMAL COMPANION\nWhen the requested NPC is an animal, beast or working companion, describe an animal rather than a human personality in disguise. Preserve its exact authored NPC type. Give it species-appropriate sensory priorities, temperament, learned commands/routines, attachments, fears, favorite places, food motivation, tolerance of crowds/noise/water and any real work it knows. A ship cat, signal pigeon, otter and watch hound should solve different problems because their bodies and training differ.\n\nBasic information should include species/type, adult or juvenile status when known, size, body condition, coat/plumage/scales, distinctive markings, eyes, scars, collar/harness/band, and how it carries itself. Hidden information may include prior handler, trauma, unusual training or a learned route, but never a gratuitous conspiracy. Personality terms should remain animal-readable—bold, skittish, food-driven, possessive of a perch, affectionate with familiar crew, noise-shy, stubborn, curious—not articulate human ideology.\n\nAnimal visuals use full-body composition and coherent zoological anatomy. Do not apply the human waist-up, sensual-feminine or masculine-beauty scaffold to them.",
    "Sapphire Wake Cultural Appearance and Name Continuity": "## CULTURE, APPEARANCE AND NAME CONTINUITY\nPreserve the supplied exact name. Never \"improve\" an established person into a more generic fantasy name. If a new name truly must be generated here, resolve upbringing first and follow the matching naming dossier; reject stock Kael/Draven/Nyx/Voss/Elara/Riven-style fantasy output, arbitrary dark/weather surnames and random apostrophes.\n\nConstruct appearance as ancestry/body plan -> cultural visual grammar -> profession/material needs -> individual taste/history. Culture should be visible through a few grounded choices of silhouette, fabric, jewellery, grooming, tattoos/markings, footwear, workwear or formalwear, but no culture is a costume uniform. Location alone does not rewrite someone's dress tradition. Foreign pieces require plausible travel, family or adoption history. Preserve existing scars, tattoos, jewellery, hair identity and recurring style. Do not make personality from culture: not every Ossaran is rigid, Velisene duplicitous, Morukai loud, Aunai mystical, Bronzewater gruff, Namarran scholarly or Tazhari flirtatious.",
    "Sapphire Wake Naming Doctrine": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE NAMING DOCTRINE — NPC DETAILS\nPreserve every supplied exact name. When a missing name truly must be generated, resolve **upbringing -> household/lineage convention -> social context -> personal name** before choosing sounds. Ancestry does not determine naming language; current location does not rewrite identity.\n\nUse the world naming dossiers as cadence anchors. Tazhari, Ossaran, Velisene, Morukai, Aunai, Bronzewater and Namarran names should remain audibly distinct without reducing them to one suffix or copying a real-world language mechanically. Crosswake/mixed-port identities require an actual family, migration, adoption or mixed-upbringing reason rather than a generic-name pool.\n\nReject generic contemporary/fantasy defaults such as **Tessa, Arlo, Finch**, Kael/Kaelen, Draven, Nyx, Voss, Elara, Riven, Thorne and Zephyr; arbitrary weather/darkness noun surnames; gratuitous apostrophes; species-coded elf/orc naming; and one-vowel evasions of the same stock pattern. The established place **The White Finch** does not make Finch a normal personal surname.\n\nTitles, ranks and epithets need provenance and do not replace the underlying personal identity. Never silently rename canon. Preserve exact spelling once established.",
    "Sapphire Wake Combat Tier and Level Discipline": "## SAPPHIRE WAKE COMBAT TIER & LEVEL DISCIPLINE\nTier and level are COMBAT statistics, not measures of narrative importance, age, fame, wealth, beauty, intelligence, professional mastery, rank, romance relevance or how detailed an NPC is. A famous innkeeper, ruler, physician, scholar, navigator, actor, companion, banker or shipwright can be average-tier while remaining socially formidable and highly competent.\n\nUse the biography first. Ordinary civilians and noncombat specialists usually belong at average tier, commonly levels 1–5. Experienced sailors, pilots, divers and field professionals without a real fighting reputation usually remain average, roughly levels 4–8. Use strong only for characters with demonstrated personal combat training or dangerous field experience such as veteran boarders, marines, raiders, duelists, hunters or captains who genuinely fight; roughly levels 6–15 is normal. Elite is rare and reserved for exceptional personal combatants, champions, major supernatural threats or famous veterans whose individual battlefield survivability is part of their canon. Boss/mythic requires an intended boss-scale or setting-defining combat threat.\n\nA title does not promote combat tier. Captain, admiral, prince, faction leader, wealthy patron or named recurring NPC does not automatically mean strong/elite. Combat level measures relevant fighting/survival threat, not years spent in a profession. Do not inflate HP to protect plot importance; use allies, law, position, escape options, reputation and story consequences instead.",
    "Sapphire Wake Maritime Official Colours": "When describing or generating government-maritime officials, keep office families visually coherent. Senior and junior officials share the same palette and symbol family, with lower ranks using simpler dress. Tazhari Princely Sea Commissioners: saffron gold, turquoise and white; symbol a turquoise wave-ring beneath a golden sun-sail or pennant. Velisene Sea Prefects: royal violet, silver and black; symbol a silver gull-mask above a black prow or oar-cross. Morukai Hearth Fleet Speakers: ember red, volcanic black and shell white; symbol a shell spiral or conch crossed with a red spear above a black wave-mark. Aunai Voyage Council Convenors: ocean teal, star white and coral accents; symbol a white star-compass over a teal reef spiral or wave path. Bronzewater League Maritime Syndics: bottle green, brass and cream; symbol a brass sextant or divider crossed with a ledger key. Namarran River Fleet Ministers: peacock blue, rich gold and ivory; symbol a golden river-crown above two blue currents. Use the palette and symbol on sashes, badges, seal cords, pennants, coats, shawls or office gear as appropriate to rank and culture.",
    "Sapphire Wake Crew Affiliation Identity": "When current context says this NPC is crew of a specific hull, preserve the exact hull and shipboard role in basicInfo/hiddenInfo as appropriate. Crew employment is separate from party membership. A newly generated 'your navigator/gunner/deckhand' is already friendly crew of that hull; an enemy officer belongs to the opposing vessel. Never leave explicit crew affiliation blank for later combat generation to guess, and never reverse it merely to supply opponents."
